<p>If you can hit a stud, use a wood screw long enough to bite about an inch
into solid timber and skip the anchor entirely &mdash; nothing you buy will beat
it. If you cannot, the anchor's job is to spread your load across as much of a
thin, brittle panel as possible. Everything below follows from that.</p>

<h2>First, work out what the wall is</h2>

<p>Drill a small pilot hole somewhere it will not matter and look at the dust.
<strong>Drywall</strong> gives fine white powder and the bit pushes through in a
few seconds into a hollow cavity. <strong>Plaster over lath</strong> gives
grittier grey dust and you will feel the bit hit wood strips behind.
<strong>Plaster over masonry</strong> and <strong>brick or block</strong> resist
hard, throw reddish or grey grit, and need a masonry bit and usually a hammer
drill. Older Yonkers buildings frequently have plaster over lath in the older
rooms and drywall in anything renovated, sometimes on the same floor.</p>

<h2>Anchors for hollow walls</h2>

