<p>Published capacities assume half-inch drywall in good condition, a correctly
sized hole and a load pulling straight down the wall rather than straight out of
it. Real capacity is lower, sometimes much lower. Treat the numbers on the
packet as a ranking rather than a promise, and derate anything that hangs out
from the wall &mdash; a bracket that projects a foot multiplies the pull-out
force at the anchor considerably.</p>

<h2>Plaster and lath deserve their own paragraph</h2>

<p>Old plaster is brittle and it crumbles rather than compresses. Expansion
anchors are a poor idea because the expanding action is what breaks the plaster
around the hole. Toggles work well because they clamp rather than expand. Drill
slowly, without hammer action, and consider a strip of painter's tape over the
spot to stop the surface chipping as the bit breaks through.</p>

