<h2>Masonry: brick, block and concrete</h2>

<p>Here the anchor grips solid material, so the choices are simpler and the
capacities much higher.</p>

<p><strong>Concrete screws</strong> (the blue ones) cut their own thread into a
pre-drilled hole and suit light and medium loads. Get the hole diameter right:
too big and the threads have nothing to cut into. <strong>Sleeve and wedge
anchors</strong> expand against the hole wall and carry serious weight. In
<strong>hollow block</strong>, aim for the solid webs rather than the voids, or
use a sleeve anchor long enough to grip the far side of the shell.</p>

<p>Two practical notes. Drill deeper than the anchor is long, because the dust
has to go somewhere &mdash; and blow or vacuum the hole out before fitting,
since packed dust is the most common reason a masonry anchor spins. And in
brick, fixing into the mortar joint is easier and weaker than fixing into the
brick itself; for anything structural, go into the brick.</p>

<h2>When to stop and find a stud</h2>

<p>Some things should not go on anchors at all. Anything overhead, anything a
person might grab or pull on, anything holding a television, and anything whose
failure would hurt somebody: find framing, or use a mounting plate spanning two
studs. A grab bar in particular is a load-bearing safety fitting and belongs on
solid backing, not on hollow-wall anchors, whatever the packet claims.</p>

<h2>Where to start</h2>

<p>The <a href="/fasteners.html">fasteners and hardware department</a> stocks
plastic plugs, threaded anchors, togglers, concrete screws and sleeve anchors,
and we sell by the piece as well as by the box &mdash; useful when a job needs
four of something rather than a hundred. Bring the old fixing or a photo of the
wall and the bracket if you are not sure; sorting it at the counter takes a
minute and saves a hole.</p>

